# Line endings
# ============================================================
#
# Without this, git on Windows prints "LF will be replaced by CRLF"
# on nearly every add/commit. Those are warnings, not errors -- but
# they're noisy enough to look like something is broken, and they
# also mean the same file can land in a diff as "every line changed"
# depending on who committed it.
#
# `* text=auto` tells git to normalise text files to LF *in the
# repository* while leaving whatever each OS wants in the working
# copy. Binary files below are marked explicitly so git never tries
# to "fix" line endings inside a PDF or an audio sample and corrupt
# it.

* text=auto

# Source and content -- always LF in the repo
*.py    text eol=lf
*.js    text eol=lf
*.css   text eol=lf
*.html  text eol=lf
*.json  text eol=lf
*.md    text eol=lf
*.yaml  text eol=lf
*.yml   text eol=lf
*.txt   text eol=lf

# Windows-only scripts genuinely need CRLF
*.bat   text eol=crlf
*.cmd   text eol=crlf

# Never touch these -- the knowledge-base PDFs and the demo audio
# sample are binary and normalising them would corrupt the files.
*.pdf   binary
*.ogg   binary
*.webm  binary
*.mp3   binary
*.wav   binary
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.jpeg  binary
*.gif   binary
*.ico   binary
*.pptx  binary
*.db    binary
